Ours (PhoneCanDo), not from llama.cpp. When a chat template refuses the
messages as sent, folds the system messages and renders once more, so a
template that allows one system message, first, takes requests with more.

Codex (Responses API) starts with two developer messages, which the server
maps to system, and Claude Code (Anthropic /v1/messages) sends its
environment block as a system message after the user turn. Qwen3.5's
template allows one system message, first, and raises "System message must
be at the beginning" otherwise, so every request from either failed.

common_chat_templates_apply, which chat completions, Responses and
Anthropic messages all reach, renders the messages as sent first: a
template that takes them (Gemma 4, Llama 3.x, Qwen3, GLM, Mistral and
others) renders exactly as before. Only if that throws (the template's
raise arrives wrapped differently per path, so any error counts) does it
render once more with the system messages folded, after developer is
mapped to system; that attempt's error, if any, is the one reported, and
everything it returns (prompt, grammar, parser) comes from the attempt
that rendered. The fold:
- the leading run of system messages becomes one, their text a blank line
  apart;
- a later one becomes "[System note]\n<text>": at the end of the user or
  tool message before it, else at the start of the user message after it,
  else (after an assistant turn) a user message of its own;
- in text parts the text joins the part next to it (templates such as
  Gemma 4's trim each part); other parts stay as they are. Text is trimmed
  first, and blank system messages are dropped.

